e often  in places like this or like moria camp  the the the former campbell  [ __ ] camp where we're here  right now um so you could interpret this  as a sign of hope perhaps but uh the  people who i talk to um they have hope  they they they they hope that uh this  sends an important signal but they are  also very aware of the difficult  situation that they're in they  appreciate that somebody like pope  francis is standing up for them but at  the same time they know that many many  others far more don't  okay well um  max as you know lesbos became a symbol  of the refugee crisis and europe's  struggle to deal with it you've of  course been on the island for a few days  talking to residents and to migrants of  course  about how things might be changing since  the crisis all began in 2015. let's have  a quick look at what you found out  the island of lesbos  a haven of tranquility with a rich  culture and a long history  aphrodite fatih  calls lesbos her home  last year her family started renting out  these bungalows to tourists hoping more  people would come back to visit  in 2015 the island became a migration  hotspot for hundreds of thousands hoping  for a better life in europe  it was extremely  difficult and challenging uh for us  and we never knew when this was going to  stop also so that was scary and we also  knew that we were helping and we knew  that we were going to pay a consequence  some kind of consequence for this at one  point  the following year tourism was down 80  percent many firms went out of business  to take pressure off the islands the  government transferred thousands of  people to the mainland today just over  two thousand people live here in the  mavervuni camp a fraction of the numbers  that used to crowd the island's previous  accommodation facility just two years  ago  despite decreasing numbers lifeview  remains very difficult  since the beginning of the pandemic  residents have only been allowed to  leave the camp once or twice a week  i cannot get good sleep i cannot get  good food i cannot get  good time so  all my time in the day all my time in  the day it's very bad because of the  situation  salat from somalia has been living in  the camp for nine months while he waits  for a decision on his asylum case he and  others are likely to be moved to another  facility soon  camps like this one which recently  opened on the island of samos are part  of the government's new strategy  improving living conditions while at the  same time keeping residents behind  fences and barbed wire  the same strategy is planned for lesbos  for very long islanders have been  resisting plans to construct a permanent  closed facility here in lesbos but after  years of arguing an agreement was  finally made to build it here  in the middle of nowhere far away from  any city or village  many islanders are still against the  planned camp  for businesswoman africifati mariola  finding a sustainable solution must  include the local population she says  it's time to show a different side of  lesbos  the stigmatization of the islands that  bear the the weight of the refugee  crisis needs to stop  we need the truth to come out there and  we need to people to realize how  beautiful and how blessed lesbos island  this is to come and see it for  themselves  for everyone here on the island the  dream of a better future remains strong  and max your report mentioned greece's  new island detention camp are they  really an improvement i mean especially  considered from the migrants perspective  um well the idea of these close  permanent camps was introduced a few  years ago when there was a large refugee  migrant population here on the island 20  000 people living in very difficult  conditions now this is this has changed  um due to depopulating the island due to  the government bringing people to the  mainland so um  right now there might be a chance for  these camps to go ahead though um we  know that uh there's still some uh some  